ZERO HTTP RESPONSE PARSER CHECKS
Date: 2026-11-18

Scope
Independent native checks of net/http-response.zero.part, composed verbatim
between the BEGIN/END ZERO HTTP RESPONSE markers in net/http-response-check.zero.
Both parser and product checker are original Zero source. No Chromium source
was used. Fixture HTTP bytes are literals and native Zero byte-generation loops.

Result
100 cases completed; 100 passed; checker process exit status 0.
Compiler: original native Zero compiler from the verified 0.12 final43 build.
Target tested here: Linux x64 native. Kernel/VM integration is a separate check.
Parser SHA-256:
f0ca0314638ad84967403d641149fd260bb7b49488be1e88cec80190450f848e

Positive coverage
HTTP/1.0 close-delimited and HTTP/1.1 Content-Length responses; mixed-case
header names; whitespace; identical and numerically equivalent duplicate
lengths; empty bodies; multiple chunks; token and quoted chunk extensions;
ignored non-framing trailers; UTF-8 and binary bodies; redirect metadata;
HTML, plain-text and other MIME kinds; 404 and unknown valid final status;
204 without framing headers; 304 with representation length/transfer metadata;
identity encoding; 255-byte Location; 12000-byte body and raw length 65535.

Negative coverage
Unsupported versions, interim responses and upgrades; malformed status lines;
bare CR/LF, NUL, DEL and non-ASCII headers; invalid field names and folding;
empty, signed, malformed, conflicting, comma-list and overflowing lengths;
short/excess bodies; ambiguous Transfer-Encoding plus Content-Length;
unsupported/duplicate transfer encoding and unsupported content compression;
duplicate Content-Type, Location and Content-Encoding; malformed, overflowing,
truncated and non-terminated chunks; missing chunk CRLF; invalid extensions;
metadata/framing fields in trailers; forbidden 204 framing; 204/304 bodies;
256-byte Location and raw length 65536.

Boundary and memory checks
8192-byte headers accepted, 8193 rejected; 128 headers/trailers accepted,
129 rejected. Every fixture checks guard words around the raw/decoded areas
and after the metadata strings. Input bytes remain unchanged. Successful
responses must publish exact status/body length/MIME/Location where specified,
exact decoded bytes and a trailing NUL. Failed responses must publish no
successful result and decoded body length zero. These are bounded canary
checks, not a formal proof of memory safety.

Issues identified and fixed before the final run
Representation Content-Length on 304 previously required a nonexistent body;
malformed empty and unterminated quoted chunk extensions were accepted;
204 framing headers were accepted; 304 Transfer-Encoding metadata incorrectly
required actual chunk data. The final parser passed all associated regressions.

Limits
This report verifies only the complete-response memory parser, using caller-
owned raw/decoded pointers at workspace offsets 688/696. It does not verify
TCP segmentation, retransmission, DNS, TLS, certificate validation, JavaScript,
CSS rendering, real website compatibility, or arbitrary malicious inputs.
The parser rejects interim 1xx, unsupported content compression and responses
above its configured bounds. UTF-8 bytes pass through; character validation
and display handling belong to the browser renderer.

Reproduction
Compile net/http-response-check.zero with the original Zero compiler --host,
then execute the produced binary. One PASS/FAIL line is printed per case;
any failed assertion causes exit status 1. The composed parser markers must
be refreshed from net/http-response.zero.part after parser changes.
